=========Some examples=========
~tcpdump -i eth0 -c 50 -v captures and displays 50 packets by listening on the eth0 interface,
which is a wired Ethernet, and displays them verbosely.
.
~tcpdump -i wlo1 -w data.pcap captures packets by listening on the wlo1 interface (the WiFi interface) and writes the packets to data.pcap.
It will continue till the user interrupts the capture by pressing CTRL-C.
.
~tcpdump -i any -nn captures packets on all interfaces and displays them on screen without domain name or protocol resolution.
.
==>host (host name OR ip addr)
Let’s say you are only interested in IP packets exchanged with your network printer or a specific game server.
ex: sudo tcpdump host example.com
==>src host (username or ip) . If you want to limit the packets to those from a particular source IP address or hostname.
==>dst host (username or ip) . Similarly, you can limit packets to those sent to a specific destination.
==> you can also specify port by typing "port (port no.)"
ex: port 80
==> you can also specify different protocols by typing its name.
ex : sudo tcpdump -i ens5 icmp -n 
==>logical operations 
~and: Captures packets where both conditions are true.
For example, tcpdump host 1.1.1.1 and tcp captures tcp traffic with host 1.1.1.1.
~or: Captures packets when either one of the conditions is true.
For instance,tcpdump udp or icmp captures UDP or ICMP traffic.
~not: Captures packets when the condition is not true. 
For example, tcpdump not tcp captures all packets except TCP segments; we expect to find UDP, ICMP, and ARP packets among the results.
